Q: what is CORS and its setting in the docker compose file

The problem CORS solves:

  When your browser loads the frontend (e.g. http://localhost:3000), and that page makes an API call to a different address (e.g. http://localhost:8082), the browser blocks it by default for security. This is called the
  same-origin policy.

  To allow it, the backend (art-user-engine) must explicitly say: "I trust requests coming from http://localhost:3000" — that's what CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS does.

  ---
  Your stack:

  Browser → http://localhost:3000  (frontend, React)
               ↓ API calls
            http://localhost:8082  (art-user-engine, Java)

  The frontend origin is http://localhost:3000, so the backend must have:
  CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

  ---
  When you deploy to a VM:

  Browser → http://192.168.1.100  (nginx serves frontend on port 80)
               ↓ API calls
            http://192.168.1.100/api  (nginx proxies to art-user-engine)

  Now the frontend origin is http://192.168.1.100, so you'd need:
  CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://192.168.1.100

  ---
  Short answer: http://localhost:3000 is correct for local development. You only need to change it when deploying to a real server — set it to whatever URL users open in their browser.


  Q2: ⏺ No. art-user-engine is a Docker-internal hostname — only other containers can reach it. The browser has no idea it exists.

  CORS is about what the browser sees in the address bar, not internal Docker networking.

  When Docker Compose is running:

  Browser opens → http://localhost  (nginx on port 80)
                       ↓  (nginx internally proxies to art-user-engine:8082)
                    art-user-engine  ← browser never sees this

  So CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS should be what the browser loads the frontend from:

  - Local machine with Compose → http://localhost
  - VM deployment → http://<vm-ip> or https://<your-domain>